# {{PROGRAM_NAME}}

A Python program for Ledgence. The application receives user-owned CloudEvent
`data`, for example `{"name": "Ada"}`, and produces
`{"message": "Hello, Ada!"}`. Execution and tracing identifiers stay in the
CloudEvent envelope. The program starts at version `0.1.0`.

## Develop and test

From this directory, with Python 3.11 or newer and uv installed:

```sh
uv sync
uv run python -m unittest discover -s tests -v
```

Alternatively, use a standard virtual environment:

```sh
python3 -m venv .venv
. .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install "ledgence-worker=={{LEDGENCE_VERSION}}"
python -m unittest discover -s tests -v
```

`ledgence-worker` provides the `ledgence.worker` authoring API, editor imports,
and local tests. It is a development dependency: the deployed Rust worker
supplies the runtime helper. Do not vendor it into the application package.
These unit tests validate the business function and handler or workflow
registration. Use a real Ledgence installation to test orchestration, retries,
waits, and durable results; calling a workflow handler directly is not a local
workflow runner.

## Choose the worker target

Edit `[target]` in `ledgence.toml` before building, unless it was configured when
the project was created. Set all three values to match the intended worker:

- `platform`: `linux/amd64` or `linux/arm64`.
- `python`: the exact CPython major/minor, such as `3.14`.
- `image`: the worker-compatible runtime image pinned as
  `repository@sha256:<64 lowercase hexadecimal digits>`.

Keep `protocol = 3`. The target is independent of your development machine;
macOS development does not imply a macOS worker. Use the same runtime image to
match native system libraries too. Placeholder values deliberately prevent a
build until the target is selected.

## Build and publish

Start a local Docker engine with Linux containers, then run:

```sh
ledgence program build
ledgence program publish --server http://127.0.0.1:8080 --register
```

Use your installation's API URL. HTTP publication must be enabled on the server.
Build prepares `.ledgence/prepared`; publish stores that immutable package;
register makes it discoverable in the program catalog. None of these commands
executes the program. Submit a task or workflow matching `[program].kind` through
the Python client or HTTP API, with `data={"name": "Ada"}`.

The selected application source is `program.py`; the builder adds the runtime
manifest and prepares dependencies from `requirements.txt`. Add application
files explicitly to `[source].include`.
Keep production dependencies in `requirements.txt`, with exact versions,
SHA-256 hashes, and all active transitive dependencies. Adding a dependency to
`pyproject.toml` for development does not include it in a deployed package.
Retain required dependency license notices. Choose the license for your own
application; this scaffold does not assign one.

Increment `[program].version` in `ledgence.toml` and `[project].version` in
`pyproject.toml` before publishing changed application bytes under a new release.
The program store does not overwrite an existing immutable version.

See the [program build and publication guide](https://docs.ledgence.com/how-to/build-and-publish-programs)
and [workflow authoring guide](https://github.com/Ledgence/ledgence/blob/develop/sdk/python/README.md)
for deployment and composition helpers.
